      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by finding the radius of the base of the original cone. The circumference of the base is given as \\(16\\pi\\) inches. The formula for the circumference of a circle is \\(2\\pi r\\), where \\(r\\) is the radius. Therefore, we can set up the equation:\n\n\\[\n2\\pi r = 16\\pi\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(r\\), we divide both sides by \\(2\\pi\\):\n\n\\[\nr = \\frac{16\\pi}{2\\pi} = 8 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the volume of the original cone. The volume \\(V\\) of a cone is given by the formula:\n\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the values \\(r = 8\\) inches and \\(h = 30\\) inches into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8)^2 (30) = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (64) (30) = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (1920) = 640\\pi \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nNow, we know that the volume of the shorter cone is \\(192\\pi\\) cubic inches. Let the height of the shorter cone be \\(h'\\). Using the volume formula for the shorter cone, we have:\n\n\\[\n192\\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8)^2 h'\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right side of the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n192\\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (64) h' = \\frac{64}{3} \\pi h'\n\\]\n\nTo find \\(h'\\), we divide both sides by \\(\\frac{64}{3} \\pi\\):\n\n\\[\nh' = \\frac{192\\pi}{\\frac{64}{3} \\pi} = \\frac{192 \\cdot 3}{64} = \\frac{576}{64} = 9 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nThe original height of the cone was 30 inches, and the shorter height is 9 inches. The ratio of the shorter height to the original height is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{9}{30} = \\frac{3}{10}\n\\]\n\nThus, the ratio of the shorter height to the original height is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{10}}\n\\]",
