      "text": "To find the ratio of the shorter height to the original height of the cone, we first need to determine the original height and the radius of the base of the cone.\n\nThe circumference of the base of the original cone is given as \\(16\\pi\\) inches. The formula for the circumference of a circle is \\(C = 2\\pi r\\), where \\(r\\) is the radius. Therefore, we can find the radius \\(r\\) of the base of the original cone as follows:\n\\[\n2\\pi r = 16\\pi \\implies r = \\frac{16\\pi}{2\\pi} = 8 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\nThe height of the original cone is given as 30 inches. The volume \\(V\\) of a right cone is given by the formula:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n\\]\nSubstituting the values of \\(r\\) and \\(h\\) for the original cone, we get:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8)^2 (30) = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 64 \\cdot 30 = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 1920 = 640\\pi \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\nNow, we know that the volume of the shorter cone is \\(192\\pi\\) cubic inches. Let the height of the shorter cone be \\(h'\\). Using the volume formula for the shorter cone, we have:\n\\[\n192\\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8)^2 h' = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 64 \\cdot h' = \\frac{64}{3} \\pi h'\n\\]\nTo find \\(h'\\), we can solve the equation:\n\\[\n192 = \\frac{64}{3} h' \\implies h' = \\frac{192 \\cdot 3}{64} = \\frac{576}{64} = 9 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\nThe ratio of the shorter height to the original height is:\n\\[\n\\frac{h'}{30} = \\frac{9}{30} = \\frac{3}{10}\n\\]\nThus, the ratio of the shorter height to the original height is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{10}}\n\\]",
